\use{document}
\use{chem}

\title{Reactions with \texttt{chem}}
\author{The texish project}
\maketitle

\section{Reaction equations}

Like mhchem, \texttt{\\ce} sets a reaction inline, flowing in the running text:
asdf \ce{CO2 + 2H2O -> CH4 + 2O2} qwer. The notation is compact and mhchem-style
— element symbols come out upright, atom counts as subscripts, a leading number as
a stoichiometric coefficient (with a thin space before its species), \texttt{+} as
a binary operator, and \texttt{->} / \texttt{<=>} as the math arrows.

Methane burns: \ce{CH4 + 2 O2 -> CO2 + 2 H2O}. The Haber process is reversible,
\ce{N2 + 3 H2 <=> 2 NH3}, and it all sits in the sentence at the text size.

To show a reaction on its own centred line, put it in \texttt{\\centerline} at a
larger size:

\bigskip
\centerline{\font lmroman 14 regular \ce{CH4 + 2 O2 -> CO2 + 2 H2O}}
\bigskip

\vfill\eject
